{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A product UX surface consumes typed product data and emits actions. Its product-truth-changing behaviour is subject to an admitted carrier contract; view-local concerns remain distinct.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":397,
          "quote":"a UX surface binds\n to one or more AssetSurface contracts",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/UX.md#UX%2F7.%20Carrier%20binding",
          "start_line":395
        }
      ],
      "key":"ux_surface",
      "label":"UX surface"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"AssetSurface is a published typed product contract providing projections and an action registry. If the product publishes no AssetSurface contracts, its design module names an equivalent typed contract to which the same binding law applies.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":416,
          "quote":"If the product does not publish AssetSurface\ncontracts, the design module names the equivalent typed contract and\napplies the same binding law to that contract.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/UX.md#UX%2F7.%20Carrier%20binding",
          "start_line":413
        }
      ],
      "key":"ux_typed_carrier",
      "label":"AssetSurface or equivalent typed carrier"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The view is a pure function from typed State to a typed ViewTree. Function, component, template, server-rendered handler, signal-derived expression and other framework shapes are possible implementations of that signature.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":491,
          "quote":"A view is a pure function from typed state to a view tree",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/UX.md#UX%2F9.%20View%20shape",
          "start_line":490
        }
      ],
      "key":"ux_view",
      "label":"Pure view"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"An executable Msg-replay proof reproduces behaviour from a recorded Msg log. The log is recorded work state and replay is resumption from it; reproducibility supplies evidence against hidden continuation inaccessible to later actors or sessions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":465,
          "quote":"the Msg log is the surface's recorded work\nstate, and replay is resumption from it",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/UX.md#UX%2F8.%20Continuation%20and%20replay%2FMsg-replay%20proof",
          "start_line":460
        }
      ],
      "key":"ux_replay_proof",
      "label":"Msg-replay proof"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The design module records project adoption, scope, stack and justification, per-surface process shapes, architectural carriers, typed bindings, effect membrane, proof surfaces, refinements and explicit exemptions before implementation where required.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":568,
          "quote":"declaring the State, Msg, Update, Cmd and Sub shapes for each UX\n   surface in the design module before implementation;",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/UX.md#UX%2F12.%20Adoption",
          "start_line":567
        }
      ],
      "key":"ux_design_module",
      "label":"UX design module"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A scaffold, prototype, debug pane or temporary proof surface can have a recorded exemption scope. Exemption is a scope, not a result or a route to production UX by accumulation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":595,
          "quote":"A scaffold, prototype, debug pane or temporary proof surface may be\nexempt only when the design module or ticket records",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/UX.md#UX%2F12.%20Adoption%2FScaffold%20and%20exemption",
          "start_line":594
        }
      ],
      "key":"ux_scaffold",
      "label":"Scaffold exemption scope"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"UX sprint compliance escrow is a short iteration window deferring eligible local UX compliance work when per-change proof cost exceeds expected correction cost. It is a cost optimisation without weakening the process model. Generic escrow and close mechanics belong to TICKETS §3 and are admitted by SPEC_METHOD §4.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":619,
          "quote":"A UX sprint may defer local UX\ncompliance proof during a short iteration window when per-change proof\ncost is higher than expected correction cost.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/UX.md#UX%2F12.%20Adoption%2FUX%20sprint%20compliance%20escrow",
          "start_line":616
        }
      ],
      "key":"ux_sprint",
      "label":"UX sprint compliance escrow"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Four predicate-specific result refinements describe surface compliance on an exact basis, replay reproduction of final State and ordered Cmd without forbidden dependencies, closure obligations for every product-meaningful interaction family and accessibility, and admission of every product-truth-changing message or action. These are source-defined evaluation domains, not evaluations performed here.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":763,
          "quote":"a review, a proof, a closure claim and a carrier\ncheck are each read for their own predicate and none is read as another",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/UX.md#UX%2F15.%20Results%20of%20this%20profile",
          "start_line":761
        }
      ],
      "key":"ux_result_domains",
      "label":"UX result refinements"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"UX source dispositions claim conservation of predecessor rows, with routes serving provenance only. Renamings are UX_METHOD/this method to this profile; DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD, SPEC_METHOD, TICKET_METHOD to DESIGN_MODULE, SPEC_METHOD, TICKETS; ODD_METHOD to graph-execution profile ODD; WORLD_MODEL_METHOD to WORLD_MODEL; method-compliance, violation and closure to their profile-relative readings. AssetSurface, workorder, graph function and ABG remain named carriers or graph terms. Old §§4–10 keep numbering; old §12 becomes §4 Realisation discretion; §§13/13A/13B become §12 and subsections; §14 becomes §13; §15 becomes §14; §8A becomes §8 Msg-replay proof. New clauses are escrow binding in the lead, §15 result refinements and §1 family table.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":826,
          "quote":"Every conserved row is restated in this member's own text; a route\nis provenance only.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/UX.md#UX%2F16.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line":825
        }
      ],
      "key":"ux_provenance_inventory",
      "label":"UX predecessor disposition inventory"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"WORLD_MODEL is an optional tier-3 profile for constructing a governed semantic layer over sovereign systems and publishing bounded artifacts. Its scope includes position and manifesto; roles and lineage imports; Builder Project specialisation; definitions, objectives, truth surface and evidence hierarchy; candidate Markov construct, effective blanket and four refinements; units and fourteen-stage flow; representation, materialisation, publication and ledgers; entity recovery, cuts and overlays; saturation, composition and projection; planes, proof, development, output, companions and result refinements. It imports FOUNDATIONS, ACTIVATION, EVALUATION, IDENTITY, SPEC_METHOD, PRODUCT_DEFINITION, RELEASE and TICKETS under PROFILES §1 without redefining their objects.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":5,
          "quote":"Tier 3. An optional profile for world-model construction: building a\ngoverned semantic layer over sovereign source systems and publishing\nbounded world-model artifacts.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL",
          "start_line":3
        }
      ],
      "key":"world_model_profile",
      "label":"WORLD_MODEL profile"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A world model is a governed semantic layer over existing authoritative source systems, descriptive before generative. It externalises expert understanding as inspectable, versioned, machine-reasonable domain truth. Its primary published semantic unit is an entity cut; generic world-model-object references normally denote that unit unless a looser candidate or intermediate is explicitly named.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":319,
          "quote":"Its primary semantic unit is the published\nentity cut (§6 Units). Generic references to a \"world-model object\" are\nshorthand for this unit unless the text explicitly names a looser\ncandidate or intermediate surface.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL%2F3.%20What%20a%20world%20model%20is",
          "start_line":316
        }
      ],
      "key":"world_model",
      "label":"World model"
    },
    {
      "conditions":[],
      "definition":"Source systems enact bounded domain function through code paths, events, records, APIs, schedules, configurations, documents and downstream obligations, and retain operational sovereignty. Domains are bounded by function and authority; records require function, provenance and treatment context for intelligibility.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":81,
          "quote":"- source systems enact domain function and remain sovereign for\n  operational truth;\n- domains are defined by bounded function and authority;\n- records are intelligible only through function, provenance and\n  treatment context;",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL%2F1.%20Position",
          "start_line":77
        }
      ],
      "key":"world_model_source_systems",
      "label":"Sovereign source systems"
    },
    {
      "conditions":[],
      "definition":"The two constitutional intents are building published domain artifacts and composing them into higher-order world models. Query, traversal and serving are delivery concerns and do not define this identity.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":99,
          "quote":"World-model work has two constitutional intents: build published domain\nartifacts; and compose those artifacts into higher-order world models",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL%2F1.%20Position%2FConstitutional%20intents",
          "start_line":95
        }
      ],
      "key":"world_model_intents",
      "label":"World-model constitutional intents"
    },
    {
      "conditions":[],
      "definition":"Material family F21 imports F2 transitions/steps/configuration for refinement, F3 results and combination, F8 identity, F9 a_c calculus and F12 project-method binding. Its instance premises, adopted rather than foundation-derived, are I1 source sovereignty and observation without replacement; I2 records lack self-contained meaning and people already commit to objects; I3 probabilistic correspondence proposals and deterministic delivery at volume; I4 some representation spaces express identity geometrically. World-model ledgers, cuts, overlays, treatments and projections are not calculus records merely because names coincide.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":161,
          "quote":"This\nprofile's ledgers, cuts, overlays, treatments and projections are its own\nconcepts and are not the calculus's records of similar name by spelling.\nIts instance premises hold where world-model work is done under this\nprofile; they are adopted with it and do not follow from the foundations",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL%2F1.%20Position%2FFoundation%20to%20law",
          "start_line":156
        }
      ],
      "key":"world_model_foundation_table",
      "label":"F21 foundation-to-law account"
    },
    {
      "conditions":[],
      "definition":"SPEC_METHOD supplies the general process constitution. WORLD_MODEL refines it for source-observing artifact-building projects and is primary within urn:stdo:bounded-context:world-model under the selected complete method basis. For graph-native world-model products, graph execution governs execution shape, traversal and product authoring while WORLD_MODEL governs semantics, publication and composition.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":239,
          "quote":"the\ngraph-execution profile (PROFILES §2) governs graph-native execution\nshape, traversal law and product-authoring structure, and this profile\ngoverns world-model semantics, publication and composition law. The two\ncompose; they do not compete",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL%2F2.%20Role%20and%20imports",
          "start_line":235
        }
      ],
      "key":"world_model_role_split",
      "label":"World-model and general-method roles"
    },
    {
      "conditions":[],
      "definition":"The exact imported identities under urn:stdo:concept:recursive-product-taxonomy: are source-project, product, install and artifact. Their source bounded context is urn:stdo:bounded-context:recursive-product-taxonomy, owned by FOUNDATIONS §2 Product lineage objects. Product means released immutable thing, possibly a builder or runtime; Install means stamped workspace instance; Artifact means published output. PRODUCT.md is the product-definition surface of a mutable Source Project, not the released Product artifact.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":270,
          "quote":"- Product retains the released immutable-thing meaning; a builder or\n  runtime may be one such Product;\n- Install retains the stamped-workspace-instance meaning;",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL%2F2.%20Role%20and%20imports",
          "start_line":263
        }
      ],
      "key":"world_model_lineage_imports",
      "label":"Imported lineage identities"
    },
    {
      "conditions":[],
      "definition":"Source Project is urn:stdo:concept:recursive-product-taxonomy:source-project, the mutable workspace building the next release cut, owned by FOUNDATIONS §2 in the recursive-product-taxonomy context.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":267,
          "quote":"- Source Project retains the\n  mutable-workspace-building-the-next-release-cut meaning;",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL%2F2.%20Role%20and%20imports",
          "start_line":266
        }
      ],
      "key":"source_project",
      "label":"Source Project"
    },
    {
      "conditions":[],
      "definition":"Builder Project, urn:stdo:concept:world-model:builder-project, is a mutable workspace configured for a world-model builder application, owning source inputs, settings and a publication lane. WORLD_MODEL §6 owns this narrower concept. It remains distinct from Release Cut, Product, Install and Artifact, and may use an installed builder Product as substrate.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":294,
          "quote":"- changed and narrowed meaning: the workspace is configured for a\n  world-model builder application and owns its source inputs, settings\n  and publication lane; it may use an installed builder Product as\n  substrate;",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL%2F2.%20Role%20and%20imports%2FBuilder%20Project%20specialisation",
          "start_line":291
        }
      ],
      "key":"builder_project",
      "label":"Builder Project"
    },
    {
      "conditions":[],
      "definition":"The published semantic layer is the one constitutional truth surface for world-model meaning. It consists of published domain artifacts, bounded fragments, entity cuts, explicit treatments, covariance edges, adjoint mappings, temporal reference artifacts and composed models built by reference. Epistemic overlays, query planes, mapping reports and compiled deterministic artifacts support it or are downstream.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":360,
          "quote":"Everything else is downstream\nor supporting: epistemic overlays, query planes, mapping reports and\ncompiled deterministic execution artifacts.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL%2F3.%20What%20a%20world%20model%20is%2FSingle%20truth%20surface",
          "start_line":357
        }
      ],
      "key":"published_semantic_layer",
      "label":"Published semantic layer"
    },
    {
      "conditions":[],
      "definition":"A Markov object is a stable self-bounding pattern in a constraint structure whose internal state can be reasoned about through its effective blanket. The substrate-neutral working claim expects such topology across biological systems, natural-language traces, learned representations and institutional systems. This is load-bearing candidate vocabulary, not an established cross-substrate theorem.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":390,
          "quote":"A Markov object is\na stable self-bounding pattern in a constraint structure whose internal\nstate can be reasoned about through its effective blanket.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL%2F4.%20The%20Markov-object%20construct",
          "start_line":383
        }
      ],
      "key":"markov_object",
      "label":"Candidate Markov-object construct"
    },
    {
      "conditions":[],
      "definition":"The effective blanket is a low-rank projection in representation space along which identity survives treatment. Attribute bases distribute identity and supply evidence, not a crisp column-membership partition: attributes outside an apparent set can still transfer partial identity, and individual firing attributes are insufficient.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":423,
          "quote":"- the effective blanket is a low-rank projection in the representation\n  space along which object identity is preserved under treatment;\n- the attribute basis is evidence for that projection; the projection is\n  not a column-membership predicate;",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL%2F4.%20The%20Markov-object%20construct%2FEffective%20blanket",
          "start_line":418
        }
      ],
      "key":"effective_blanket",
      "label":"Effective blanket"
    },
    {
      "conditions":[],
      "definition":"An Entity Cut is the primary published semantic unit: an identity-bearing, bounded, evidence-backed immutable selection from the attribute ledger. It publishes identity, boundary, authority, state, evidence, treatment, covariance and ambiguity, each traced to ledger entries and assurance. It is lawful without an identity direction.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":645,
          "quote":"An entity cut is lawful\nwithout an identity direction. It publishes identity, boundary,\nauthority, state, evidence, treatment, covariance and ambiguity, each\ntraced to ledger entries and their assurance basis.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL%2F6.%20Units",
          "start_line":642
        }
      ],
      "key":"entity_cut",
      "label":"Entity Cut"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["A declared representation space exists."],
      "definition":"A Markov-Object Overlay is an optional geometric addition to an entity cut, containing identity direction, projection support and effective coordinate, constructed and verified under §10. It strengthens the cut; its absence does not weaken the cut. Overlay publication remains candidate-class unless a genuine promotion-gate result supports a formally closed kind.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":613,
          "quote":"an optional geometric overlay on an entity cut, admissible only where a declared representation space exists; it adds an identity direction, projection support and effective coordinate, constructed and verified under §10; the overlay strengthens an entity cut, and its absence does not weaken one",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL%2F6.%20Units",
          "start_line":613
        }
      ],
      "key":"markov_overlay",
      "label":"Markov-Object Overlay"
    },
    {
      "conditions":[],
      "definition":"The Attribute Ledger is an append-only ordered record of qualified attribute claims over stable object identity, and the immediate semantic source of entity cuts. Entries can contain observations, derivations, interpreted meanings, constraints, relations, provenance, authority and effective, observed and publication coordinates.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":610,
          "quote":"an append-only ordered record of qualified attribute claims over a stable object identity",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL%2F6.%20Units",
          "start_line":610
        }
      ],
      "key":"attribute_ledger",
      "label":"Attribute Ledger"
    },
    {
      "conditions":[],
      "definition":"Assurance is the governed basis for accepting a traced source observation as a qualified semantic claim, explaining why it is lawful world-model truth rather than merely an observation.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":609,
          "quote":"the governed basis on which a traced source observation is accepted as a qualified semantic claim",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL%2F6.%20Units",
          "start_line":609
        }
      ],
      "key":"world_model_assurance",
      "label":"Assurance"
    },
    {
      "conditions":[],
      "definition":"Other named units are App, a world-model builder application; Published Domain Artifact, an immutable published builder output; Fragment, a bounded published slice within it; World Model Object, intentionally loose shorthand useful during recovery and discussion; Treatment Surface, semantic reinterpretation between domains; Covariance Edge, declared correspondence of object changes; Adjoint Mapping, a treatment's interpret-back contract; Temporal Reference Artifact, a changing value/code/classification set; Composed World Model, a reference-based stitching preserving identity, version and local authority; Projection, a downstream artifact derived from the semantic kernel; and optional Query Plane, serving without constitutional publication authority.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":620,
          "quote":"| Projection | a user-facing or downstream-facing artifact derived from the semantic kernel | P1 |\n| Query Plane | an optional downstream serving surface that makes published domain artifacts or composed world models queryable or traversable without becoming the constitutional publication authority | P6 |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL%2F6.%20Units",
          "start_line":619
        }
      ],
      "key":"world_model_unit_catalog",
      "label":"World-model artifact and relation units"
    },
    {
      "conditions":[],
      "definition":"The publication ledger records immutable published cuts of domain artifacts and object projections; the attribute ledger records qualified semantic claims over object identity. In the initial lane Git and the filesystem suffice for publication, while a later database complements them as query plane.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":786,
          "quote":"This publication ledger is distinct from the attribute ledger: the\nattribute ledger records qualified semantic claims over object identity,\nand the publication ledger records immutable published cuts of domain\nartifacts and object projections.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL%2F8.%20Representation%20and%20publication%2FPublication%20law",
          "start_line":783
        }
      ],
      "key":"publication_ledger",
      "label":"Publication ledger"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["The builder uses a declared representation space in which identity is a geometric direction."],
      "definition":"An identity direction is a low-rank geometric combination over columns, not an attribute column. Its canonical extraction is d(object) = μ(evidence_object) − μ(evidence_null), taken across contexts.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":910,
          "quote":"`d(object) = μ(evidence_object) − μ(evidence_null)`",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL.md#WORLD_MODEL%2F10.%20Markov-object%20overlay%20construction%2FIdentity-direction%20extraction",
          "start_line":910
        }
      ],
      "key":"identity_direction",
      "label":"Identity direction"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["A candidate appears in multiple contexts."],
      "definition":"Invariant core consists of attributes whose contribution to identity direction is stable across ≥ N of N contexts; context coat consists of attributes selected by a specific context, carrying reinterpretation. Both are evidence: core carries identity and coat carries treatment. Coat-to-core ratio characterises rather than grades quality.",
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":954,
          "quote":"The core carries\nidentity; the coat carries treatment. The coat to core ratio is a\ncharacterisation, not a quality metric",
